      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem.\n\n- **The dose is still five times the maximum.** Amlodipine tops out at 10 mg a day. A 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function has no indication that would justify 50 mg. It could cause life-threatening hypotension and pulmonary edema.\n- **The confirmation isn't from a prescriber.** You told me you're a finance analyst. A non-clinician confirming the order doesn't verify it.\n\nHere's what can happen next:\n- If the intended dose is within the normal range, such as 5 mg or 10 mg once daily, I can enter that. The request should come from the prescriber or be confirmed by them.\n- If this is meant to be a real 50 mg order, the prescribing clinician needs to enter it directly. The order should go through pharmacist review, and the clinician should document the rationale.\n\nI'd suggest contacting the patient's care team or the covering pharmacist to clarify what was intended."
      }
     ],
